<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<lint>
    <!--
      Both entries are scoped exemptions, not a baseline. A baseline lets suppressed debt accumulate
      unseen and pins each finding to a path that carries a version, so it needs regenerating on
      every dependency bump; a scoped ignore keeps the check live for everything else and survives
      bumps. See build-logic's AndroidLint.kt for the policy.
    -->

    <!--
      False positive. Every class it names IS declared in AndroidManifest.xml, the apps install and
      launch, and the check still reports each one a dozen times over. Measured 2026-09-14: writing
      the manifest names fully qualified instead of relative changes nothing, so this is not the
      debug build type's applicationIdSuffix resolving `.MainActivity` against the wrong package.
      What is left is the source layout — these modules keep Kotlin in src/main/kotlin rather than
      the src/main/java this check still assumes — but that was not proven, so the reason stays
      open. Worth re-testing on an AGP bump: delete this block and see whether it comes back.

      Scoped to the two classes rather than the check disabled, so a genuinely unregistered Activity
      or Service is still caught.
    -->
    <issue id="Registered">
        <ignore regexp="Abit(Wear)?Application"/>
        <ignore regexp="MainActivity"/>
    </issue>

    <!--
      The launcher rasters are PNG on purpose, and lint is right that WebP would be smaller. They
      are not there for the launcher, which takes the adaptive icon in mipmap-anydpi and never reads
      them. They exist for Firebase App Distribution, which does not parse adaptive-icon XML and
      falls back to a generated monogram when it cannot find a flat raster it understands.

      Scoped to these files, so ConvertToWebp keeps flagging every other image in the project, where
      the size argument does apply.
    -->
    <issue id="ConvertToWebp">
        <ignore regexp="mipmap-[^/]+/ic_launcher.*\.png"/>
    </issue>
    <!--
      gRPC, which Firestore pulls in, references javax.naming from a JNDI-based DNS resolver that
      never runs on Android — the class is only reachable on a JVM where JNDI exists. There is no
      way to exclude the code and no newer gRPC without it.

      Scoped to the one artifact rather than baselined: a baseline pins the finding to the jar's
      cache path, which embeds the version and its hash, so every Firebase bump would unmatch the
      entry and fail the build twice over. Matching on the artifact name survives version bumps, and
      InvalidPackage stays live for every other dependency.
    -->
    <issue id="InvalidPackage">
        <ignore regexp="grpc-core"/>
    </issue>

    <!--
      GoogleSignIn resolves default_web_client_id by name rather than through R, and lint is right
      that reflection defeats build-time verification. It is deliberate here: that resource only
      exists once a web OAuth client does, so referencing it through R would stop a checkout without
      one from compiling at all — which is every fresh clone of this repository.

      Scoped to the one file, so DiscouragedApi keeps flagging reflection anywhere else.
    -->
    <issue id="DiscouragedApi">
        <ignore regexp="GoogleSignIn.kt"/>
    </issue>
</lint>
